jros2: a thread that reaches a reader of this process takes its reference in one step

With intraprocess delivery a writer calls a reader of this process through a LocalReaderPointer::Instance. Two races
let it use a reader that another thread has deleted, or read a shared pointer that another thread resets; both crash
the JVM when publishers and subscriptions of one process are created and deleted at the same time.

RefCountedPointer::Instance read is_active_ and the pointer first and only then incremented the count, under the mutex.
deactivate() could run in between, find a count of 0 and let the reader be deleted, and the instance then called it.
The count and the pointer are now taken in one critical section, so deactivate() either stops the instance from getting
the pointer or waits for it.

StatefulWriter::intraprocess_heartbeat() called reader_proxy->local_reader() without the writer's lock. On the initial
heartbeat event of a ReaderProxy that runs on the event thread while ReaderProxy::stop(), under the lock, resets
ReaderLocator::local_reader_. It now takes the lock first, like every other caller of local_reader().

